Agentic search incurs higher per-query costs due to sequential model steps for file exploration but eliminates upfront indexing expenses and ensures maximum freshness by reading the current file state without re-indexing, whereas RAG requires initial embedding/compute costs for maintenance and risks staleness if indexes are not updated. Regarding accuracy on exact error codes (e.g., `ERR-4471`), agentic search using regex reliably retrieves precise identifiers, while vector-based RAG often fails by retrieving semantically similar but incorrect codes (e.g., `ERR-4417`) because embeddings treat such strings as nearly identical. Additionally, RAG accuracy is further compromised when error codes span chunk boundaries, whereas agentic search avoids this specific retrieval limitation.

**Citations:**
*   **Costs:** Agentic search costs grow with model steps [agentic-search.md:line 2]; RAG has upfront indexing costs [rag-overview.md:line 2].
*   **Freshness:** Internal docs with agentic search avoid re-indexing overhead [agentic-search.md:line 1]; RAG indexes go stale requiring maintenance [freshness.md:lines 1-2; rag-overview.md:line 3]. Agentic search ensures fresh results via on-demand reading [agentic-search.md:1; freshness.md:2].
*   **Accuracy:** Exact matches work well with agentic (regex) search [agentic-search.md:2]; embeddings treat similar codes as identical causing errors [error-codes.md:2]; stale indexes in RAG reduce accuracy while fresh agent reads do not [freshness.md:2]; chunking issues affect RAG even with exact lookup attempts [rag-overview.md:2].
